<p>It is not a cure-all. There are tasks for which a one-person kitchen is enough, and separately there are tasks where multi shines.</p>

<h3>Harness = running the whole kitchen</h3>
<p>Who does what and when is designed in advance, and agents · skills · orchestration are tightly connected. <strong>Managing that workflow and those rules well overall</strong> is harness engineering.</p>

<h3>Quality has to be refined continuously</h3>
<p>You have to <strong>review and give feedback both midway and at the end</strong> &mdash; whether the ingredients are fresh (the intermediate outputs) and whether the final dish is fine. Even with a harness, <strong>a finished product does not come out in one shot.</strong> Treat what comes out as a draft, and keep refining the agents to raise it to high quality.</p>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">Token usage</span>
    Because you run it multi, token usage goes up. It grows especially when <strong>the agents talk to each other</strong>, and in measurements there were cases of using <strong>about 30% more</strong>.
</div>
